WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.930
This next story is really interesting because
it combines politics with

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:12.170
and with legality and people have been wondering
on our subreddit and phone calls that I've

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:18.780
received, if Nancy Pelosi wanted to, could
she wait to send the articles of impeachment

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.090
to the Senate for a trial until a more fair
trial has been agreed to with Senate majority

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.460
leader Mitch McConnell, or merely for political
reasons.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.869
Like, if, if Nancy Pelosi says we'd be better
off starting this thing in June when Trump

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:32.169
will be debating the democratic nominee.

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:36.140
Oh, much more, much more, uh, promptly, uh,
soon after.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.230
Could she do that?

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:41.469
And Mitch McConnell, of course, has said,
it's not going to be a fair trial.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:46.399
He said he will openly coordinate the trial
with Donald Trump, uh, letting the accused

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:50.969
run their own trial or at least influence
it that, uh, Mitch McConnell is not an impartial

00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:53.720
juror as required by the constitution.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.629
So there's the question, what if Nancy Pelosi
waits and negotiates?

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:01.629
And that is exactly what she now said is going
to happen.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:07.630
Nancy Pelosi last night saying, after Donald
Trump had been impeached quote, we have legislation

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:12.340
approved by the rules committee that will
enable us to decide how we will send over

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.079
the articles of impeachment.

00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:20.759
We cannot name impeachment managers until
we see what the process is on the Senate side.

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:26.530
This may actually be a pretty slick way of
getting some concessions from Republicans

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:33.259
in the Senate or if not of simply, uh, having
the trial at a time that would be more disadvantageous

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.259
to Donald Trump.

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:38.700
Because remember, Trump has already talked
about stretching the trial as long as possible

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.770
because he believes that would be advantageous
to him.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.910
You know, as he sees it.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:48.310
It may be true, it may not be the longer the
delay before the trial starts, the longer

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:53.270
that Trump is just an impeached president,
not an acquitted president.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.270
That's interesting.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.469
And of course the longer that the delay goes,
the more likely it is to start overlapping

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:00.539
not only with democratic primaries.

00:02:00.539 --> 00:02:04.200
If it goes long enough, it'll start overlapping
with general election debates.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:10.189
You could conceivably have Donald Trump, uh,
doing campaign spending, buying campaign ads

00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:14.180
at the same time that there is an impeachment
trial going on, which is almost like free

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.579
money for advertising from the other side.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:19.390
The fact that there would be an impeachment
trial going on.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:25.140
I don't think that the trial happening closer
to election day is good for Trump as a big

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.900
picture thing, aside from his followers, who
of course will be enraged by the mere fact

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:30.900
that the trial exists.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.709
You know, the more casual voter, the tens
of millions of people who vote, but they don't

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:37.270
follow politics so closely.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.099
If it's getting close to election day and
they know the president is under impeachment

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:47.689
trial, that is going to dissolve some votes
that would otherwise have gone to Donald Trump.

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:52.620
That is the leverage that Nancy Pelosi now
has with the idea of delaying when she's going

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.840
to send the articles of impeachment over to
the Senate.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.590
Now, once again, we're speculating about what
the impact might be, but we are once again

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.260
in unprecedented territory here.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:07.099
At a minimum, this throws off Republicans
and Trump because the plan they had Mitch

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:11.859
McConnell's plan was to immediately do a kangaroo
court, which this is what they've been saying

00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:13.030
has already gone on.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.109
The real kangaroo court would be the Senate
trial.

00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:21.359
Mitch McConnell wants, maybe drag it out,
maybe not, but get the headlines through the

00:03:21.359 --> 00:03:24.780
media and then move on with claims of vindication.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.670
Now instead, it's headlines of Trump is impeached.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.569
Trump is impeached, and these are going to
go on for a while without the benefit of the

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:36.260
vindication storylines that would almost certainly
come after an acquittal.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:42.330
The narrative has been taken from Republicans
and is now Trump has been impeached period,

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.790
and that will stay the narrative until Nancy
Pelosi decides she's ready to send impeachment

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.310
articles to the Senate.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:53.219
Trump wants this to move on so that he can
be acquitted and gloat, and now he's going

00:03:53.219 --> 00:03:59.709
to have to sit and sit and tweet and sit as
an impeached president, and now Trump is tweeting

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:01.670
that Nancy simply can't do this.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.840
Saying, quote this morning, I got impeached
last night without one Republican vote being

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.420
cast with the do nothing.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.519
Dems on their continuation of the greatest
witch hunt in American history.

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:12.519
Now the do nothing.

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:17.930
Dems want to do nothing with the articles
and not deliver them to the Senate, but it's

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.780
Senate's call.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.660
Does an impeached president have international
credibility?

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.319
Among other world leaders.

00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:26.319
I don't know.

00:04:26.319 --> 00:04:28.000
It doesn't sound like it's too good for Trump.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.419
What's that phrase that Don jr used?

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:35.419
Um, about the Russian lawyer meeting something
like if it's what you say, I love it, especially

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:36.900
later in the summer.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.400
I think it applies doubly here, particularly
if this thing even conceivably stretches,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.569
uh, beyond spring and at some point we will
figure out what the timing of it is.

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:48.289
But uh, I think there are many criticisms
of Nancy Pelosi.

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:54.120
I do think it was a mistake not to include
emoluments in the impeachment articles, but

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.729
this is pretty slick and I think it may actually
work even if it only works to send Trump again

00:04:58.729 --> 00:05:00.120
off the rails.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:13.389
That probably in and of itself

00:05:13.389 --> 00:06:21.610
is worthwhile.

